{{if .Candidate}}# Working on {{.Name}}

This is the explicit, unreleased candidate scoped-record profile. Read pluto.toml
and README.md. Run pluto fmt, pluto openapi, pluto architecture check --directory . --format json,
and pluto vet. Keep every file <=1,000 lines and preserve authored migrations.

All production Go is managed, including domain, operation workflow, narrow ports,
SQL adapters, transport and composition. Do not edit generated source or add hooks,
initializers, alternate handlers, raw SQL clients, reflection or unsafe escapes.
Extend the framework's supported contract when business rules need a new pattern.
Use pluto add blueprint --file contracts.toml for atomic declaration changes.

Schema 10 owns independent operation authority and resource/domain/data contracts.
Each protected use case shares one opaque identity/resource gate across HTTP,
worker and host-approved agent entry. Never create identity from actor/role strings
or trust payload authority. Current verified sessions, tenant assignments, role
permissions and authoritative resource facts are refreshed before protected work.
Roles are explicit catalog declarations; adding operations never assigns users.

Commands normalize once in the owning domain constructor. Private records,
rehydration and machine construction stay in their managed owners. State decisions
are pure; evaluated changes bind to the exact machine/scope/entity/source/version.
The data adapter atomically commits mutation, audit, typed outbox work and required
idempotency completion. Pending or uncertain claims require reconciliation; never
steal them or retry with a new logical key. Queries apply visibility before LIMIT,
use stable scoped cursors and reject oversized exports. Cache display snapshots
only after fresh authorization; lifecycle guards use authoritative SQL facts.

Workers use configured credentials and typed bounded delivery, not payload actor
claims. Agent writes use host-issued opaque prepared intents and actual execution
receipts. Model text never proves a write occurred. HTTP, worker and tool adapters
must invoke the same sealed operation. Provider URLs are deployment configuration;
external delivery remains at-least-once and requires stable-key deduplication.

Apply explicit database migrations before startup. No constructor creates schemas,
users, role assignments or fixture credentials. Candidate dependencies are explicit
local replacements; do not invent release versions or silently switch profiles.
Never log tokens, bodies, secrets, private records or provider payloads.
